## 8. Conclusion

Schrödinger's 1918 transposition was not a mathematical trick; it was a physical revelation that Einstein tragically suppressed due to his philosophical commitment to Mach's principle. By shifting the cosmological constant to the source side of the equation, Schrödinger exposed the fundamental duality of space-time: gravity binds, and anti-gravity separates.

We have demonstrated that the contemporary 
Λ
ΛCDM framework has mistaken its own parametric flexibility for predictive power. The successes of dark matter and dark energy are not rooted in accurate accounting; they are rooted in the misattribution of observable geometric anomalies to unobservable, post-hoc entities. The anti-gravity model, derived directly from the structural symmetry of Einstein's equations, accounts for galactic rotation, void dynamics, and cosmic acceleration without invoking a single new particle or fine-tuned constant.

Einstein called Schrödinger's move a "Pandora's Box." In a sense, he was right—it opens a box that allows us to throw away the ghosts of dark matter and dark energy and return physics to a geometrically grounded, deterministic reality. The Schrödinger Anticipation is not merely a historical footnote; it is the key to resolving the current cosmological crisis.
